<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project DefaultTargets="Build" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">

  <!--
    The MSBuild half of the two-build-system seam.

    CMake owns the engine and src/host/; this project owns XAML, MIDL and cppwinrt, none of which
    CMake can drive. The direction is one-way on purpose - this file reads a props file CMake
    generates, and CMake never invokes MSBuild - because the reverse would be a cycle neither
    could break. See src/CMakeLists.txt, which writes the props, and build.ps1, which runs both
    halves in the one order that works.
  -->

  <ItemGroup Label="ProjectConfigurations">
    <ProjectConfiguration Include="Debug|x64">
      <Configuration>Debug</Configuration>
      <Platform>x64</Platform>
    </ProjectConfiguration>
    <ProjectConfiguration Include="Release|x64">
      <Configuration>Release</Configuration>
      <Platform>x64</Platform>
    </ProjectConfiguration>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    x64 only, and deliberately. vcpkg.json declares "supports": "windows & x64" for the same reason:
    the engine is verified on one architecture here, and an arm64 configuration nobody builds is a
    configuration nobody notices has rotted. Adding arm64 later means re-running M1's fidelity
    check on that target, not adding a line to this file.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup Label="Globals">
    <CppWinRTOptimized>true</CppWinRTOptimized>
    <CppWinRTRootNamespaceAutoMerge>true</CppWinRTRootNamespaceAutoMerge>
    <CppWinRTGenerateWindowsMetadata>true</CppWinRTGenerateWindowsMetadata>
    <MinimalCoreWin>true</MinimalCoreWin>
    <ProjectGuid>{A7F3C2E1-5B84-4D96-9C17-2E8B6D4A0F53}</ProjectGuid>
    <ProjectName>WindowsTSPlayer</ProjectName>
    <RootNamespace>WindowsTSPlayer</RootNamespace>
    <DefaultLanguage>en-US</DefaultLanguage>
    <MinimumVisualStudioVersion>17.0</MinimumVisualStudioVersion>

    <!--
      These three lines look contradictory and are not. Together they say "a desktop process, built
      by the Store toolchain, that is not sandboxed", which is exactly what a WinUI 3 desktop app is.

      ApplicationType is what pulls in the C++ XAML build. Microsoft.Cpp.Default.props sets
      WindowsStoreApp from it directly, WindowsStoreApp is what imports
      Microsoft.Cpp.AppContainerApplication.props, and that props file is the only thing that points
      _CppCommonExtensionTargets at MSBuild\Microsoft\WindowsXaml\v18.0\Microsoft.Windows.UI.Xaml.Cpp.targets.
      Without it MarkupCompilePass1 and Pass2 never run: cppwinrt still generates MainWindow.g.h from
      the IDL, so the build gets far enough to look healthy, and then fails on a missing
      App.xaml.g.h and on every x:Name in the window being an undeclared identifier.

      AppContainerApplication must be here and must be false, and it is not a restatement of the
      default. Microsoft.Cpp.Default.props defaults it to *true* whenever WindowsPackageType is MSIX,
      which is set below. Letting that stand would sandbox the process, and this program cannot be
      sandboxed: Session::load_song opens songs with a plain std::ifstream on a raw filesystem path,
      shared verbatim with the Apple and Linux front ends, and an AppContainer refuses that open.
    -->
    <AppContainerApplication>false</AppContainerApplication>
    <ApplicationType>Windows Store</ApplicationType>

    <!--
      The bill for ApplicationType, paid here.

      AppContainerApplication.props also sets TargetPlatformIdentifier to UAP, from which
      Microsoft.NuGet.targets derives NuGetTargetMoniker "UAP,Version=v10.0". NuGet, meanwhile,
      restores any .vcxproj as "native,Version=v0.0" no matter what the project says. The two
      disagree and the build stops with

          error : Your project does not reference "UAP,Version=v10.0" framework.
          Add a reference to "UAP,Version=v10.0" in the "TargetFrameworks" property

      naming a framework nothing here ever asked for and suggesting a property that does not exist in
      a C++ project. Pinning the moniker to what restore actually wrote is the whole fix.

      Not <UseTargetPlatformAsNuGetTargetMoniker>false</UseTargetPlatformAsNuGetTargetMoniker>, which
      is the other obvious lever and does not work: it only falls back to TargetFrameworkMoniker,
      which by then is .NETCore,Version=v5.0 and no closer to matching.
    -->
    <NuGetTargetMoniker>native,Version=v0.0</NuGetTargetMoniker>

    <!--
      The second half of the same bill. With TargetPlatformIdentifier at UAP, Microsoft.NuGet.targets
      derives a runtime identifier of win10-x64 and then insists the project restore for it:

          error : Your project file doesn't list 'win10-x64' as a "RuntimeIdentifier".

      For a non-UAP project it would have defaulted RuntimeIdentifiers to win;win-x86;win-x64;win-arm64
      by itself; under UAP it defaults nothing and errors instead. Declaring the one identifier this
      program is ever built for settles it, and matches the x64-only ProjectConfigurations above.
    -->
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>win10-x64</RuntimeIdentifiers>

    <WindowsTargetPlatformVersion>10.0.26100.0</WindowsTargetPlatformVersion>
    <WindowsTargetPlatformMinVersion>10.0.19041.0</WindowsTargetPlatformMinVersion>

    <!--
      The CMake half selects its own Windows SDK and currently lands on 10.0.28000. The two halves
      disagreeing is harmless *here* and only here: the static libraries expose nothing but our own
      headers and the C++ standard library across the seam, so no Windows SDK type crosses it. What
      must agree is the CRT, which is the Debug/Release mapping below, not the SDK.
    -->

    <UseWinUI>true</UseWinUI>
    <EnableMsixTooling>true</EnableMsixTooling>
    <WindowsPackageType>MSIX</WindowsPackageType>

    <!-- Restore writes a lock file, and it is committed. Without one, "the same commit" can restore
         different transitive package versions on two machines, and the Windows App SDK has a deep
         dependency graph - ten sibling packages, several of which float. -->
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Import Project="$(VCTargetsPath)\Microsoft.Cpp.Default.props" />

  <PropertyGroup Label="Configuration">
    <ConfigurationType>Application</ConfigurationType>
    <PlatformToolset>v145</PlatformToolset>
    <CharacterSet>Unicode</CharacterSet>
    <UseDebugLibraries Condition="'$(Configuration)'=='Debug'">true</UseDebugLibraries>
    <UseDebugLibraries Condition="'$(Configuration)'!='Debug'">false</UseDebugLibraries>
    <WholeProgramOptimization>false</WholeProgramOptimization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Never link-time-code-generate this program, which is why WholeProgramOptimization is pinned off
    above rather than left to the Release default. The engine's control path is 16-bit fixed point
    and depends on wrapping, and its float-narrowing guarantees break if a*b+c is fused into an FMA.
    /fp:precise arrives on the engine's own targets through ts::tabulasonora; LTO would let the
    linker re-derive across that boundary anyway. Same rule as CLAUDE.md's: not a preference.
  -->

  <Import Project="$(VCTargetsPath)\Microsoft.Cpp.props" />

  <!-- ============================================================================================
       The seam.

       CMake's Debug maps to MSBuild's Debug and CMake's RelWithDebInfo to MSBuild's Release, and
       that mapping is what keeps the CRT consistent. A Debug app linking a RelWithDebInfo host
       layer is LNK2038 _ITERATOR_DEBUG_LEVEL mismatch - the single most likely way to break this
       build, and an error that names neither file.

       The preset differs from the configuration because the CMake half also carries
       TSGUI_FAST_DEBUG: the `dev` preset compiles the engine at /O2 while leaving our sources at
       /Od, because at /Od the synth renders at ~1.4x realtime and no ring can absorb that.
       ============================================================================================ -->
  <PropertyGroup Label="TsguiSeam">
    <TsguiCMakePreset Condition="'$(Configuration)'=='Debug'">dev</TsguiCMakePreset>
    <TsguiCMakeConfig Condition="'$(Configuration)'=='Debug'">Debug</TsguiCMakeConfig>
    <TsguiCMakePreset Condition="'$(Configuration)'!='Debug'">release</TsguiCMakePreset>
    <TsguiCMakeConfig Condition="'$(Configuration)'!='Debug'">RelWithDebInfo</TsguiCMakeConfig>
    <TsguiHostProps>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\build\$(TsguiCMakePreset)\TsguiHost.$(TsguiCMakeConfig).props</TsguiHostProps>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Import Project="$(TsguiHostProps)" Condition="Exists('$(TsguiHostProps)')" />

  <!--
    Fail loudly and early rather than at link time. Running only this half is the mistake build.ps1
    exists to prevent, and without this target the symptom is a wall of unresolved externals that
    says nothing about the actual cause.
  -->
  <Target Name="TsguiRequireHostProps" BeforeTargets="PrepareForBuild">
    <Error Condition="!Exists('$(TsguiHostProps)')"
           Text="The CMake half has not been built for this configuration.

  Missing: $(TsguiHostProps)

  Run the CMake half first - or just use build.ps1, which runs both in order:

      cmake --preset $(TsguiCMakePreset)
      cmake --build --preset $(TsguiCMakePreset)
" />
  </Target>

  <ItemDefinitionGroup>
    <ClCompile>
      <PrecompiledHeader>Use</PrecompiledHeader>
      <PrecompiledHeaderFile>pch.h</PrecompiledHeaderFile>
      <LanguageStandard>stdcpp20</LanguageStandard>
      <WarningLevel>Level4</WarningLevel>
      <ConformanceMode>true</ConformanceMode>
      <AdditionalIncludeDirectories>$(ProjectDir);$(GeneratedFilesDir);$(IntDir);%(AdditionalIncludeDirectories)</AdditionalIncludeDirectories>

      <!--
        WINRT_LEAN_AND_MEAN trims the projection headers and WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN trims windows.h.
        NOMINMAX is the one that is not merely hygiene: the host layer and the engine both use
        std::min and std::max in headers this project includes, and windows.h's macros of those names
        would turn every one of them into a syntax error.
      -->
      <PreprocessorDefinitions>NOMINMAX;WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN;WINRT_LEAN_AND_MEAN;DISABLE_XAML_GENERATED_MAIN;%(PreprocessorDefinitions)</PreprocessorDefinitions>

      <!--
        C4100 is unreferenced formal parameter, which every XAML event handler signature produces by
        construction - sender and args are usually both unused. Silenced at /W4 rather than dropping
        to /W3, so the warnings that matter stay on.
      -->
      <DisableSpecificWarnings>4100;%(DisableSpecificWarnings)</DisableSpecificWarnings>

      <!--
        Source and execution character sets both UTF-8. MSVC otherwise reads a file with no BOM in
        the system code page, which quietly mangles any non-ASCII literal on a machine whose ACP is
        not UTF-8 - and the sources here carry the middle dots that join clauses on a mixer strip,
        the em dash a part with no name shows, and Segoe MDL2 glyph codepoints.

        This is the compile-time half of the pair. app.manifest declares the *process* code page as
        UTF-8, which is what makes narrow Win32 and CRT calls interpret paths correctly at runtime.
        Neither implies the other and this program needs both.
      -->
      <AdditionalOptions>/utf-8 %(AdditionalOptions)</AdditionalOptions>
    </ClCompile>
    <Link>
      <SubSystem>Windows</SubSystem>
      <GenerateDebugInformation>true</GenerateDebugInformation>
    </Link>
  </ItemDefinitionGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ClCompile Include="pch.cpp">
      <PrecompiledHeader>Create</PrecompiledHeader>
    </ClCompile>
    <ClCompile Include="App.xaml.cpp">
      <DependentUpon>App.xaml</DependentUpon>
    </ClCompile>
    <ClCompile Include="Activation.cpp" />
    <ClCompile Include="MainWindow.xaml.cpp">
      <DependentUpon>MainWindow.xaml</DependentUpon>
    </ClCompile>
    <ClCompile Include="PartModel.cpp" />
    <ClCompile Include="PlayerModel.cpp" />
    <ClCompile Include="MediaControls.cpp" />
    <ClCompile Include="PrefsDialog.cpp" />
    <ClCompile Include="SettingsRows.cpp" />
    <ClCompile Include="SettingsStore.cpp" />
    <ClCompile Include="SongInfoWindow.cpp" />
    <ClCompile Include="WindowChrome.cpp" />
    <ClCompile Include="MixerView.xaml.cpp">
      <DependentUpon>MixerView.xaml</DependentUpon>
    </ClCompile>
    <ClCompile Include="PartRow.xaml.cpp">
      <DependentUpon>PartRow.xaml</DependentUpon>
    </ClCompile>
    <ClCompile Include="VoiceMeter.xaml.cpp">
      <DependentUpon>VoiceMeter.xaml</DependentUpon>
    </ClCompile>
    <ClCompile Include="TransportView.xaml.cpp">
      <DependentUpon>TransportView.xaml</DependentUpon>
    </ClCompile>

    <!--
      cppwinrt generates module.g.cpp and then leaves it to the project to compile: its targets add
      the XAML metadata provider and the C++ module interfaces to ClCompile, but never this. It
      holds the module's activation glue, and without it the link fails on exactly two symbols,

          XamlTypeInfo.g.obj : error LNK2019: unresolved external symbol WINRT_CanUnloadNow
          XamlTypeInfo.g.obj : error LNK2019: unresolved external symbol WINRT_GetActivationFactory

      referenced from the designer entry points the XAML compiler emits.

      Listing a file that does not exist yet is deliberate and safe. It is written by
      CppWinRTMakeComponentProjection, which runs well before ClCompile; MSBuild does not check that
      a non-wildcard item exists at evaluation time, so the path resolves by the time the compiler is
      handed it. A wildcard here would *not* work, because that is evaluated too early.
    -->
    <ClCompile Include="$(GeneratedFilesDir)module.g.cpp" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ClInclude Include="pch.h" />
    <ClInclude Include="App.xaml.h">
      <DependentUpon>App.xaml</DependentUpon>
    </ClInclude>
    <ClInclude Include="MainWindow.xaml.h">
      <DependentUpon>MainWindow.xaml</DependentUpon>
    </ClInclude>
    <ClInclude Include="PartModel.h" />
    <ClInclude Include="PlayerModel.h" />
    <ClInclude Include="Activation.h" />
    <ClInclude Include="MediaControls.h" />
    <ClInclude Include="PrefsDialog.h" />
    <ClInclude Include="SettingsRows.h" />
    <ClInclude Include="SettingsStore.h" />
    <ClInclude Include="SongFormats.h" />
    <ClInclude Include="SongInfoWindow.h" />
    <ClInclude Include="WindowChrome.h" />
    <ClInclude Include="MixerView.xaml.h">
      <DependentUpon>MixerView.xaml</DependentUpon>
    </ClInclude>
    <ClInclude Include="PartRow.xaml.h">
      <DependentUpon>PartRow.xaml</DependentUpon>
    </ClInclude>
    <ClInclude Include="VoiceMeter.xaml.h">
      <DependentUpon>VoiceMeter.xaml</DependentUpon>
    </ClInclude>
    <ClInclude Include="TransportView.xaml.h">
      <DependentUpon>TransportView.xaml</DependentUpon>
    </ClInclude>
    <ClInclude Include="ToneMap.h" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Midl Include="App.idl" />
    <Midl Include="MainWindow.idl" />
    <Midl Include="PartModel.idl" />
    <Midl Include="PlayerModel.idl" />
    <Midl Include="MixerView.idl" />
    <Midl Include="PartRow.idl" />
    <Midl Include="VoiceMeter.idl" />
    <Midl Include="TransportView.idl" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ApplicationDefinition Include="App.xaml" />
    <Page Include="MainWindow.xaml" />
    <Page Include="MixerView.xaml" />
    <Page Include="PartRow.xaml" />
    <Page Include="VoiceMeter.xaml" />
    <Page Include="TransportView.xaml" />
    <Page Include="Styles.xaml" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Manifest Include="app.manifest" />
    <AppxManifest Include="Package.appxmanifest">
      <SubType>Designer</SubType>
    </AppxManifest>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    A wildcard, not a list. tools\Convert-Icons.ps1 emits fifty scale- and targetsize-qualified
    variants and deletes whatever was there before, so an enumerated list here would have to be
    edited every time the set changed and would silently drop assets when it was not. The files
    exist on disk before the project is loaded, which is the condition a wildcard needs.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Image Include="Assets\*.png" />

    <!--
      The window icon, which the tiles do not cover: the title bar, the Alt-Tab card and the system
      menu take theirs from the HWND, and WinUI 3 sets none from the package.

      An Image item, like the tiles beside it, because that is the item type this project system
      actually packages.
    -->
    <Image Include="Assets\AppIcon.ico" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    NOTICE.md and LICENSE ship inside the package, and this is not tidiness. The reverb and chorus
    coefficients compiled into the engine are Roland-derived, and NativeTS requires the notice to
    travel with any binary built from it. A package without these two files is a licensing problem,
    not a missing nicety.

    None with CopyToOutputDirectory, not Content, and the difference is not cosmetic. In a C++ MSIX
    build the packaging targets read @(Content) for exactly four special files - the manifest, the
    layout file, the store association and the bundle mapping - and ignore it otherwise. What
    actually reaches the package is whatever GetCopyToOutputDirectoryItemsOutputGroup returns. The
    obvious spelling therefore shipped nothing, silently, and a build that omits the notice looks
    identical to one that includes it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\NOTICE.md">
      <Link>NOTICE.md</Link>
      <DeploymentContent>true</DeploymentContent>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </None>
    <None Include="..\..\LICENSE">
      <Link>LICENSE</Link>
      <DeploymentContent>true</DeploymentContent>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Pinned to exact versions, with a committed packages.lock.json beside them. "Latest" is how a
    build that worked yesterday stops working today for reasons no commit explains.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Windows.CppWinRT" Version="[3.0.260715.1]" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK" Version="[2.4.0]" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Windows.SDK.BuildTools" Version="[10.0.28000.2526]" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Windows.ImplementationLibrary" Version="[1.0.260126.7]" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <Import Project="$(VCTargetsPath)\Microsoft.Cpp.targets" />

</Project>
